DirectComposition swapchains that present into the host window (JUCE 8, etc).

Ported from github.com/giang17/wine (branch d2d1-dcomp-11.17), the audio-focused
Wine fork implementing real DXGI composition swap chains for plugin GUI
frameworks. Wine's own CreateSwapChainForComposition fakes a hidden window;
presenting to it jams in the GL layer and deadlocks the application's renderer
(the Spitfire Audio app froze on its sign-in screen). This gives the composition
swapchain a real backing window, and the dcomp compositor re-targets that
swapchain to the hosting window so frames GL-present straight into it instead of
being blitted through a bitmap.

Three layers:
- dxgi: a WineDCompSwapchain backing window; WM_WINE_DCOMP_SET_TARGET retargets
  the swapchain to the composition target and chooses GL vs GDI present.
- wined3d + win32u + includes: keep-back-buffers swapchains, the dcomp leaf
  layer, the GL present path, and the window/opengl plumbing they need.
- dcomp: when a visual's content is a composition swapchain, post
  WM_WINE_DCOMP_SET_TARGET to its backing window (located via the desktop-window
  property dxgi publishes) instead of blitting. POSTED not sent -- the composite
  thread holds dcomp_cs and a sync send to the app thread deadlocks.

Supersedes the earlier hidden-window and WaitForVBlank-pacing patches. Layers on
dcomp-steinberg (needs its real dcomp device). Verified: Spitfire Audio reaches
and holds its sign-in UI; HALion Sonic 7, SWAM (incl. overlay dialogs) and the
Audio Modeling Software Center render with no regression, cold session, concurrent.

